[{:db/id 17592186203967 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9413e565-6aa1-548a-b350-e9d6b541e888" "question-4403992c-2eff-5cc7-8e99-ee2d1e59b055" "question-911c5b80-ac30-5c55-987b-932a9ae84c63" "question-f3772bbb-ddc0-5270-93da-efa4049fbb34" "question-b01871bf-cff8-540f-8321-0bc0568904bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203677 :question/problem "Use the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n![](images/14/14cdf48e441388f15954ecb2ffbe7e56d653abb1dfaa3d95b2abd573e6318cca.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e60ebf4ac02326cff610f1d4238bb2b8c16062aaa8a50c7cc040631e65d3a5b0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes split into sixths.\n\n![](images/6c/6c4328991ef5f89f91b3df5b4d43f8034d58249442700d59c2d842a44c9e8cda.png)\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$ sixths in each group, with $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ remaining.\n\n![](images/e0/e08a28fee137c40615d7261b6282dce8dd0392084a8d801f9cc848364a3ad0f5.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $\\color{red}2$ full groups.\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{5}{6} = {\\color{red}{2}} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203968 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d489db84-f688-528a-b633-ad7594d23057" "question-5c17e7c1-7039-5ee7-ba75-14d302d2f636" "question-7ff8bb9b-dc96-57ee-9dbf-d8dad14fd153" "question-b3177db0-b185-5ef2-96e9-e6878521ef6d" "question-68cff8ab-d96c-583e-af11-a7fb638626de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203679 :question/problem "![](images/60/60034ce6b9e7e7e5cd24c8f6f2f3d80a1bbc5d3a8532f3c4ccaeb3d89eaaeb1d.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}{\\color{blue}7}$, we first need to divide each of the $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}7$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/a3/a373e9e5c6a5dc23240029d2d89fef66f4b9959ffef349f5ea351fffc897d3c8.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$:\n\n![](images/1c/1cfe8f5a08ee9464489cacef94b0d94982def065a9ab290acc9ebbec6efc7bf7.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\fbox{4}$ full groups.\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ pieces left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = \\fbox{4} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9413e565-6aa1-548a-b350-e9d6b541e888" :question/id #uuid "9413e565-6aa1-548a-b350-e9d6b541e888" :question/math-academy-id "q-237489" :question/problem "![](images/18/18919dad766971de40e8d9e204ac6dc0a811fc3d8857e87e776636f839e83eaa.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{3}{4}=1\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $1$ whole split into quarters.\n\n![](images/a2/a2d50ab7d44607b616d4332abcd114b489462e72843ce90b6405a23364917759.png)\n\n- There are $3$ quarters in each group, with $1$ remaining.\n\n![](images/7b/7bda99628d44b1474fc7416e1a6ceb3e79291e85bbfa337a05b4d778e592c062.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There is $1$ full group.\n\n- There is $1$ quarter left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{3}{4}=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.d